<chapter xml:id="ch-group-features">
  <title>Grouping Features</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The previous chapter used baseline features to make item-level patterns
      visible. Several of its displays also pointed beyond the baseline
      comparison. A placement heatmap is marginal, so it cannot show which
      <xref ref="gt-card" text="custom">cards</xref> a student grouped together
      (<xref ref="subsec-heatmap-interpretation" text="title"/>). The error-profile
      display can include grouping bars only after those grouping features have
      been defined (<xref ref="subsec-error-profile-bars" text="title"/>). The
      label-free preview raised the question of whether a wrong labeled answer
      may nevertheless have the right grouping structure
      (<xref ref="subsec-mislabel-gap-preview" text="title"/>). This chapter
      supplies those definitions.
    </p>

    <p>
      The stance remains the same as in the baseline chapter. The platform grades a
      <xref ref="gt-check" text="custom">check</xref> by comparing the submitted
      <xref ref="gt-configuration" text="custom">configuration</xref> to the
      <xref ref="gt-key" text="custom">key</xref> exactly
      (<xref ref="sec-feedback-policy"/>): a card is correct only in its home
      zone. Thus the grader, and the outcome layer of the log, is
      <term>label-aware</term>. The baseline agreement score
      <m>\sigma(X)=\langle X,X^\star\rangle_F</m> remains the standard correctness
      feature. The features developed here are diagnostics. They ask questions the
      labeled comparison cannot answer, such as whether a wrong response has the
      right groups under the wrong names, or whether two equally distant responses
      differ in grouping structure.
    </p>

    <p>
      The chapter develops five families of comparison features:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>label-free agreement</term>, which asks whether a response becomes
            correct after renaming the authored slots;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>partition views</term>, which read a configuration as a grouping
            of cards, with or without zone labels;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>merge and split features</term>, which identify key groups that
            have been combined or divided;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>pairwise agreement metrics</term>, which summarize grouping
            agreement over card pairs; and
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>weighted and semantic distances</term>, which keep the labeled
            comparison but allow errors to carry unequal costs.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      A motivating section first separates labeled accuracy from grouping
      structure. The closing section then collects the feature families and their
      intended uses.
    </p>

    <p>
      These features should be used deliberately. Each changes what
      <q>close to the key</q> means. Label-free and partition-based features are
      appropriate when the item design or research question concerns grouping
      structure, not when exact labeled placement is the only intended outcome.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-why-labeled-accuracy-is-not-enough">
    <title>Why Labeled Accuracy Is Not Enough</title>

    <p>
      The baseline key distance
      <m>d^\star(X)=d_{\mathcal{X}}(X,X^\star)=P-\sigma(X)</m> counts the cards
      not in their home zones. It is a labeled comparison: card <m>j</m> is correct
      only when it occupies the exact zone <m>s^\star(j)</m> specified by the key.
    </p>

    <p>
      Labeled accuracy is often exactly the right comparison. If an item asks
      students to sort statements into named mathematical categories, then a card
      placed under the wrong category name should count as wrong. The label is part
      of the answer. This is also the comparison the student experiences: every
      Correct or Incorrect returned by the grader is a verdict about labeled
      placement.
    </p>

    <p>
      But labeled accuracy can collapse structurally different answers to the same
      number. For the running shapes question, whose key is
      <m>s^\star=(1,1,2,0)</m>, consider the checked response
      <md>
        s=(2,2,1,0).
      </md>
      The student has placed the two triangles together, the square alone, and the
      circle in the <xref ref="gt-tray" text="custom">tray</xref>. As a grouping of
      cards, this response matches the key. As a labeled placement, it is almost
      entirely wrong because the two occupied slot names have been swapped: only
      the circle is correct, so <m>\sigma(X)=1</m> and <m>d^\star(X)=3</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The example separates three notions of comparison:
      <dl width="medium">
        <li>
          <title>labeled placement</title>
          <p>
            Does each card occupy its exact home zone? This is the baseline
            comparison and the grader's comparison.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>label-free placement</title>
          <p>
            Could the authored slots be renamed so that the response matches the
            key?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>grouping structure</title>
          <p>
            Which cards are grouped together, regardless of what those groups are
            called?
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      The next section quantifies label-free placement. The two sections after that
      define grouping structure and its merge, split, and pairwise summaries.
      Throughout, <m>\sigma</m> remains the standard label-aware score; the
      label-free score <m>\widetilde{\sigma}</m> is a diagnostic; and the gap
      <m>\widetilde{\sigma}-\sigma</m> measures how much of the labeled error can
      be repaired by renaming slots. The final comparison family keeps the labeled
      comparison but changes the cost attached to different errors.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-label-free-agreement">
    <title>Label-Free Agreement</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Label-free agreement compares a response to the key after allowing the
        authored slot labels to be renamed. The tray is not renamed: being unplaced
        is a status of a card, not a name attached to a group. Thus the comparison
        preserves the distinction between placed and unplaced cards while ignoring
        the names of the authored slots.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-slot-permutations">
      <title>Slot Permutations and the Label-Free Score</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>S_R</m> be the set of permutations of the authored slots
        <m>\{1,\dots,R\}</m>. In this section, a permutation
        <m>\pi\in S_R</m> is read as a matching from key slots to response slots:
        <m>\pi(b)=a</m> means that response slot <m>z_a</m> is being treated as the
        candidate for key slot <m>z_b</m>. The tray is fixed. Equivalently,
        <m>\pi</m> extends to the full zone set by <m>\pi(0)=0</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        This matching can also be written as a row permutation. Let
        <m>Q_\pi</m> be the permutation matrix satisfying
        <md>
          Q_\pi e_0=e_0,
          \qquad
          Q_\pi e_{\pi(b)}=e_b
          \quad (1\leq b\leq R),
        </md>
        where <m>e_0,\dots,e_R</m> are the zone-row basis vectors. Multiplying
        <m>X</m> by <m>Q_\pi</m> moves the matched response rows into the key-row
        positions while leaving the tray row fixed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <term>label-free agreement score</term> is the best agreement with the
        key obtainable by such a slot matching:
        <md xml:id="an-label-free-agreement">
          \widetilde{\sigma}(X)
          =
          \max_{\pi\in S_R}
          \left\langle Q_\pi X, X^\star\right\rangle_F.
        </md>
        The identity permutation is allowed, so
        <m>\widetilde{\sigma}(X)\geq\sigma(X)</m> for every state.
      </p>

      <p>
        The corresponding <term>label-free key distance</term> is
        <md xml:id="an-label-free-distance">
          \widetilde{d}^{\star}(X)
          =
          P-\widetilde{\sigma}(X).
        </md>
        It counts the cards that remain wrong even after the most favorable
        renaming of the slots. Thus
        <m>\widetilde{d}^{\star}(X)\leq d^\star(X)</m> always.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-label-free-example">
      <title>The Running Example</title>

      <p>
        For the swapped-slots response
        <m>s=(2,2,1,0)</m> against the key <m>s^\star=(1,1,2,0)</m>, exchanging
        the roles of slots <m>z_1</m> and <m>z_2</m> turns the response into the
        key. Therefore
        <md>
          \sigma(X)=1,
          \qquad
          \widetilde{\sigma}(X)=4,
          \qquad
          \widetilde{d}^{\star}(X)=0.
        </md>
        The response is label-free correct even though it is labeled almost
        entirely wrong.
      </p>

      <p>
        Renaming slots cannot repair every error. The first checked state of the
        running <xref ref="gt-attempt" text="custom">attempt</xref>,
        <m>s=(1,1,2,3)</m>, places the circle, a
        <xref ref="gt-decoy-card" text="custom">decoy card</xref>, in the Pentagon
        slot. No permutation of slot labels returns a placed card to the tray, so
        here
        <md>
          \widetilde{\sigma}(X)=\sigma(X)=3.
        </md>
        Label-free agreement forgives naming errors among authored slots. It does
        not forgive omissions, <xref ref="gt-decoy-card-leak" text="custom">decoy-card leaks</xref>,
        or any error that crosses the tray boundary.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-computing-label-free-agreement">
      <title>Computing Label-Free Agreement</title>

      <p>
        The maximum in <xref ref="an-label-free-agreement" text="custom">
        <m>\widetilde{\sigma}</m></xref> can be computed from the zone confusion
        matrix <m>M(X)=XX^{\star\top}</m>. Its entry <m>M_{ih}(X)</m> counts the
        cards currently in zone <m>z_i</m> whose home zone is <m>z_h</m>. Under a
        slot matching <m>\pi</m>, the credited cards are the tray-to-tray cards
        plus, for each key slot <m>b</m>, the cards in the matched response slot
        <m>\pi(b)</m>. Hence
        <md xml:id="an-label-free-assignment">
          \widetilde{\sigma}(X)
          =
          M_{00}(X)
          +
          \max_{\pi\in S_R}
          \sum_{b=1}^{R} M_{\pi(b)b}(X).
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        For small items, the maximum can be found by checking all <m>R!</m>
        permutations. For larger items, it is a maximum-weight assignment problem,
        solvable exactly by standard assignment algorithms. The computation uses
        the slot-by-slot part of <m>M</m>, which the next section names and uses as
        the common input for grouping diagnostics.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-when-label-free-is-appropriate">
      <title>The Labeling Gap</title>

      <p>
        Reporting the labeled and label-free distances together is more informative
        than reporting either alone. Their difference, the <term>labeling gap</term>,
        is
        <md xml:id="an-labeling-gap">
          d^\star(X)-\widetilde{d}^{\star}(X)
          =
          \widetilde{\sigma}(X)-\sigma(X).
        </md>
        It is the number of labeled errors repaired by the best slot renaming. The
        swapped-slots response has gap <m>3</m>, all of its labeled error. The
        decoy-leak response above has gap <m>0</m>, because renaming explains none
        of the error.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-mislabel-gap">
        <caption>
          The relabel gain <m>\widetilde{\sigma} - \sigma</m>, the amount a
          best&#8209;case renaming of the slots would add to the score. Bars at a
          positive gap whose cards are <em>grouped</em> correctly (highlighted)
          are pure labeling errors: the student sorted the cards into the right
          piles but attached the wrong names. The figure is drawn from the same
          example cohort as the displays of <xref ref="ch-visualization-diagnostics"/>.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-mislabel_gap.png" width="70%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Histogram of the label&#8209;free minus label&#8209;exact score, with
              bars split by whether the grouping is already perfect.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        At cohort scale, the gap produces the display previewed in the previous
        chapter: a histogram of <m>\widetilde{\sigma}-\sigma</m> over checked
        states, optionally split by whether the grouping is already perfect. A
        cohort with many positive gaps and perfect groupings is misnaming
        categories it has formed. That is different from a cohort that has not
        formed the categories at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        Whether label-free agreement should be reported as an outcome, rather than
        only as a diagnostic, depends on the item. When visible slot labels carry
        mathematical content, the labeled score is the appropriate correctness
        feature, and <m>\widetilde{\sigma}</m> would hide real errors. Label-free
        agreement should stand in for correctness only when the research question
        concerns grouping alone and that decision is stated explicitly.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-grouping-and-partition-views">
    <title>Grouping and Partition Views</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Label-free agreement still matches slots to slots. A further step drops
        slot names entirely and keeps only the grouping they induce: two cards are
        together when they occupy the same zone. This partition view is the common
        foundation for the merge and split features and the pairwise metrics of the
        next two sections.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-state-induced-partition">
      <title>The Partition Induced by a State</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>s:\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{Z}</m> be the configuration represented by
        the membership matrix <m>X=X(s)</m>. The state defines an equivalence
        relation on cards by
        <md xml:id="an-state-induced-equivalence">
          j\sim_X \ell
          \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
          s(j)=s(\ell).
        </md>
        The induced partition of <m>\mathcal{P}</m> is denoted <m>\Pi_X</m>. Its
        nonempty blocks are
        <md xml:id="an-state-induced-blocks">
          B_i(X)=\{j\in\mathcal{P}:s(j)=i\},
          \qquad
          i\in\mathcal{Z}.
        </md>
        The key induces the key partition <m>\Pi^\star=\Pi_{X^\star}</m>. Comparing
        <m>\Pi_X</m> with <m>\Pi^\star</m> asks whether the response grouped the
        cards as the key does, regardless of where the groups sit.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-marked-and-unmarked-partitions">
      <title>Marked and Unmarked Partitions, and the Tray</title>

      <p>
        Two partition views are useful. A <term>marked partition</term> remembers
        which block belongs to which zone. It therefore retains the labeled content
        of the configuration. An <term>unmarked partition</term> remembers only
        which cards are together. The marked view is appropriate when slot labels
        are part of the answer. The unmarked view is appropriate when the question
        is whether students formed the right groups.
      </p>

      <p>
        The tray requires special treatment in either view. It does not usually
        represent a group the student formed; it holds cards the student has not
        placed. A decoy card and a not-yet-placed required card may both sit in the
        tray, but for different reasons. Therefore the grouping comparisons below
        treat the tray boundary as distinguished even when authored slot labels are
        treated as interchangeable. By default, the grouping features compare only
        cards that the response and the key both place in authored slots. Omissions
        and decoy-card leaks are boundary errors and are already counted card by
        card by the baseline error profile.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-partition-confusion-table">
      <title>The Zone Confusion Matrix and Its Slot Block Submatrix</title>

      <p>
        The zone confusion matrix <m>M(X)=XX^{\star\top}</m> is the contingency
        table of the response and key partitions. Its entry <m>M_{ih}(X)</m>
        counts the cards in response block <m>B_i(X)</m> and key block
        <m>B_h(X^\star)</m>. Its margins are the current and key occupancy vectors.
      </p>

      <p>
        The grouping comparisons use the <term>slot block</term>, the submatrix
        obtained by dropping the tray row and tray column:
        <md xml:id="an-slot-confusion-submatrix">
          N(X)=\bigl(M_{ab}(X)\bigr)_{1\leq a,b\leq R},
          \qquad
          N_{ab}(X)
          =
          \#\{j: s(j)=a,\ s^\star(j)=b\}.
        </md>
        The entry <m>N_{ab}(X)</m> counts cards the student placed in slot
        <m>z_a</m> whose home slot is <m>z_b</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The cards counted by <m>N(X)</m> are the <term>co-placed cards</term>,
        <md xml:id="an-co-placed-cards">
          \mathcal{P}_{\operatorname{slot}}(X)
          =
          \{j\in\mathcal{P}:s(j)>0\text{ and }s^\star(j)>0\}.
        </md>
        We write
        <md xml:id="an-co-placed-count">
          n(X)=\#\mathcal{P}_{\operatorname{slot}}(X)
          =
          \sum_{a=1}^{R}\sum_{b=1}^{R}N_{ab}(X).
        </md>
        Cards in the tray row of <m>M</m> are omissions. Cards in the tray column
        are decoy cards. They are excluded from <m>N</m> because they are boundary
        errors, not slot-grouping structure.
      </p>

      <p>
        The slot block is the input shared by the rest of the chapter. The label-free 
        assignment of <xref ref="an-label-free-assignment" text="custom">the previous
        section</xref> maximizes over its entries, merge and split features look for 
        non-zero values in its rows and columns, and the Rand family and variation of 
        information use its entries and margins.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-merge-and-split-features">
    <title>Merge and Split Features</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Merge and split features name two structural ways a student response grouping 
        can disagree with the key grouping. A merge combines cards from different key
        groups. A split divides cards from one key group. These are descriptions of 
        student response structure; they do not by themselves explain why the student
        produced that structure.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-merge-features">
      <title>Merges</title>

      <p>
        A <term>merge</term> occurs when a student response slot contains at least two 
        cards from different key slots. A response slot, <m>z_a</m>, is called a <term>merge 
        slot</term> when it contains at least two cards from different key slots. 
        This amounts to counting the number of non-zero entries in the <m>a</m>-th row 
        of the slot block
        <md xml:id="an-row-support-size">
          \#\{b : N_{ab}(X)>0\}
        </md>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two summaries are useful. The number of merge slots is
        <md xml:id="an-merge-zone-count">
          N_{\operatorname{merge}}(X)
          =
          \#\bigl\{a : \#\{b:N_{ab}(X)>0\}\geq 2\bigr\},
        </md>
        and the <term>merge excess</term> is
        <md xml:id="an-merge-excess">
          E_{\operatorname{merge}}(X)
          =
          \sum_{a=1}^{R} \max\bigl\{0,\ \#\{b:N_{ab}(X)>0\}-1\bigr\}
        </md>.
        The excess counts how many extra key cards, beyond the first, are in the set 
        of student slots. For example, a student slot containing three key cards from 
        three different key groups has an excess of <m>2</m>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-split-features">
      <title>Splits</title>

      <p>
        A <term>split</term> accounts for cards from one key slot that are
        distributed across several response slots. A key slot, <m>z_b</m>, is said to 
        be <term>split</term> when at least two of its cards lie in different response 
        slots. In this case we count the non-zero entries in the <m>b</m>-th column of 
        the slot block
        <md xml:id="an-column-support-size">
          \#\{a : N_{ab}(X)>0\}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The corresponding summaries are the number of split key slots,
        <md xml:id="an-split-key-block-count">
          N_{\operatorname{split}}(X)
          =
          \#\bigl\{b : \#\{a:N_{ab}(X)>0\}\geq 2\bigr\},
        </md>
        and the <term>split excess</term>,
        <md xml:id="an-split-excess">
          E_{\operatorname{split}}(X)
          =
          \sum_{b=1}^{R} \max\bigl\{0,\ \#\{a:N_{ab}(X)>0\}-1\bigr\}.
        </md>
        The excess counts how many additional response slots were used beyond the
        first response slot for each key group.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-merge-split-example">
      <title>The Running Example</title>

      <p>
        For the key <m>s^\star=(1,1,2,0)</m>, the response
        <md>
          s=(1,1,1,0)
        </md>
        piles the square in with the two triangles. The first row of its slot block
        is <m>(2,1,0)</m>: response slot <m>z_1</m> draws on two key slots. Hence
        <m>N_{\operatorname{merge}}=E_{\operatorname{merge}}=1</m>, while no key
        group is split.
      </p>

      <p>
        The response
        <md>
          s=(1,2,2,0)
        </md>
        leaves one triangle in <m>z_1</m> and moves the other in with the square.
        The triangle key group is split across <m>z_1</m> and <m>z_2</m>. At the
        same time, response slot <m>z_2</m> merges the stray triangle with the
        square, so
        <m>E_{\operatorname{merge}}=E_{\operatorname{split}}=1</m>. With a two-card
        key group, this pairing is forced: splitting the pair sends one card
        elsewhere, and if that destination is occupied, a merge appears with the
        split. The larger example of <xref ref="subsec-five-card-variant" text="title"/>
        shows the two errors more clearly.
      </p>

      <p>
        Finally, the first checked state of the running attempt,
        <m>s=(1,1,2,3)</m>, has no merges or splits. Its slot block is diagonal.
        Its errors occur at the tray boundary, which is the point of the next
        subsection.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-grouping-versus-baseline-profile">
      <title>Merge and Split Counts Are Not the Error Profile</title>

      <p>
        The baseline error profile
        <md>
          \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}(X)
          =
          (O,W_{\operatorname{slot}},L_{\mathcal{P}},L_{\mathcal{R}})
        </md>
        and the merge and split counts describe wrong answers along different
        axes. The baseline profile classifies misplaced cards by boundary type:
        left in the tray, placed in the wrong authored slot, or involved in a
        decoy error. Its first three components partition the misplaced cards and
        sum to <m>d^\star(X)</m>. Merge and split counts instead describe relations
        among co-placed cards. They ignore the tray, disregard slot names, and do
        not decompose the key distance.
      </p>

      <p>
        The running example separates the axes. The decoy-leak state <m>(1,1,2,3)</m> 
        has 
        <md>
          \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}=(0,0,1,1)
        </md>
        but no merges or splits: it is wrong at the tray boundary, with perfect grouping 
        among the co-placed cards. The split state <m>(1,2,2,0)</m> has 
        <md>
          \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}=(0,1,0,0)
        </md>,
        a single wrong-slot placement, but both a merge and a split. One moved card has 
        created two grouping relations that disagree with the key. Neither summary 
        determines the other, so displays should label their units.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-tray-in-merge-split">
      <title>Treating the Tray</title>

      <p>
        Reading merges and splits from the slot block <m>N(X)</m> excludes the tray
        by construction. This is the right default: the baseline profile already
        counts omissions and decoy-card leaks card by card, and counting the tray as
        another group would let a single omission masquerade as a split of its key
        group.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some analyses may intentionally treat unplaced cards as a response
        category. For example, an item prompt might make <q>leave it out</q> one of
        the classifications. In that setting, the same non-zero entry counts can be
        computed from the full confusion matrix <m>M(X)</m>, including the tray
        row and column. That choice turns omissions and decoy-card leaks into
        grouping structure, so it should be stated explicitly rather than adopted
        silently.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-pairwise-agreement-metrics">
    <title>Pairwise Agreement Metrics</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Merge and split counts locate grouping errors but do not measure their size. 
        A slot that merges two singletons and a slot that merges two four-card groups 
        both count once. Pairwise metrics supply magnitudes by comparing the response 
        and key groupings over unordered pairs of co-placed cards, then compressing 
        the comparison into one index.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-pair-counts">
      <title>Pair Counts</title>

      <p>
        Fix a state <m>X</m> and let <m>n=n(X)</m> be the number of co-placed cards.
        Each unordered pair of distinct co-placed cards falls into one of four
        categories, according to whether the response groups the pair together and
        whether the key groups the pair together.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-pair-counts">
        <title>Pair categories for comparing the response and key groupings.</title>
        <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
          <col width="22%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="36%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="42%" halign="left"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Category</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Condition</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Interpretation</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>grouped pair</p></cell>
            <cell><p>together in response, together in key</p></cell>
            <cell><p>A pair that belongs together has been kept together.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>merge pair</p></cell>
            <cell><p>together in response, apart in key</p></cell>
            <cell><p>A pair from different key groups has been merged.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>split pair</p></cell>
            <cell><p>apart in response, together in key</p></cell>
            <cell><p>A pair from one key group has been separated.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>separated pair</p></cell>
            <cell><p>apart in response, apart in key</p></cell>
            <cell><p>A pair that belongs apart has been kept apart.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Grouped and separated pairs are in pairwise-agreement; merge and split pairs 
        are in key-disagreement. The four counts sum to <m>\binom{n}{2}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The counts are computed from the slot block. Write <m>N_{a\ \cdot}</m> and
        <m>N_{\cdot\ b}</m> for the row and column sums of <m>N(X)</m>. The numbers
        of pairs together in the response, together in the key, and together in
        both are
        <md xml:id="an-pair-together-counts">
          T_X=\sum_{a=1}^{R}\binom{N_{a\ \cdot}}{2},
          \qquad
          T_\star=\sum_{b=1}^{R}\binom{N_{\cdot\ b}}{2},
          \qquad
          T_{X\star}=\sum_{a,b=1}^{R}\binom{N_{ab}}{2}.
        </md>
        Hence
        <md>
          \#\{\text{merge pairs}\}=T_X-T_{X\star},
          \qquad
          \#\{\text{split pairs}\}=T_\star-T_{X\star},
        </md>
        and the pairwise-agreement count is
        <m>\binom{n}{2}-T_X-T_\star+2T_{X\star}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pairwise metrics are undefined or uninformative when <m>n&lt;2</m>, because
        there are no co-placed pairs to compare. In that case they should be
        reported as missing rather than as zero.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-rand-index">
      <title>Rand Index</title>

      <p>
        For <m>n\geq 2</m>, the <term>Rand index</term> is the pairwise-agreement fraction:
        <md xml:id="rand-index">
          RI(X)
          =
          \frac{\binom{n}{2}-T_X-T_\star+2T_{X\star}}{\binom{n}{2}}.
        </md>
        A value of <m>1</m> means the response and key groupings agree on every
        co-placed pair. Smaller values mean fewer pairwise agreements.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Rand index is simple but can be inflated by separated pairs. When an
        item has many small key groups, most pairs belong apart, and many responses
        keep most of those pairs apart. The adjusted Rand index removes this block
        size baseline.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-adjusted-rand-index">
      <title>Adjusted Rand Index</title>

      <p>
        The <term>adjusted Rand index</term> corrects for the agreement expected
        from random groupings with the same block sizes. In terms of the
        <xref ref="an-pair-together-counts" text="custom">together-counts</xref>,
        the expected value of <m>T_{X\star}</m> under random matching is
        <m>T_XT_\star/\binom{n}{2}</m>, and
        <md xml:id="an-adjusted-rand-index">
          ARI(X)
          =
          \frac{T_{X\star}-\dfrac{T_XT_\star}{\binom{n}{2}}}
          {\dfrac{1}{2}\bigl(T_X+T_\star\bigr)-\dfrac{T_XT_\star}{\binom{n}{2}}}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The adjusted index equals <m>1</m> when the groupings agree exactly, is near
        <m>0</m> at the chance baseline, and can be negative when agreement falls
        below that baseline. If the denominator is zero, the index is degenerate;
        the analysis should report that case rather than forcing a numeric value.
        Because the correction depends on block sizes, the adjusted index is
        preferable to the Rand index when comparing items or cohorts with different
        group-size profiles.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-variation-of-information">
      <title>Variation of Information</title>

      <p>
        Information theory gives a distance-like comparison between groupings. Let
        <m>\Pi</m> and <m>\Pi^\star</m> be the response and key partitions of the
        co-placed cards, with block sizes given by the margins of <m>N(X)</m>. For
        <m>n>0</m>, define
        <md>
          p_{ab}=\frac{N_{ab}}{n},
          \qquad
          p_{a\ \cdot}=\frac{N_{a\ \cdot}}{n},
          \qquad
          p_{\cdot\ b}=\frac{N_{\cdot\ b}}{n}.
        </md>
        The <term>variation of information</term> is
        <md xml:id="an-variation-of-information">
          VI(\Pi,\Pi^\star)
          =
          H(\Pi)+H(\Pi^\star)-2I(\Pi;\Pi^\star),
        </md>
        where
        <md>
          H(\Pi)=-\sum_{a} p_{a\ \cdot}\log(p_{a\ \cdot}),
          \qquad
          H(\Pi^\star)=-\sum_{b} p_{\cdot\ b}\log(p_{\cdot\ b}),
        </md>
        and
        <md>
          I(\Pi;\Pi^\star)
          =
          \sum_{a,b}
          p_{ab}\log\left(\frac{p_{ab}}{p_{a\ \cdot}\,p_{\cdot\ b}}\right).
        </md>
        Terms with zero probability are omitted, equivalently using the convention
        <m>0\log(0)=0</m>. Any logarithm base may be used if reported; the examples
        below use base <m>2</m>, so the units are bits.
      </p>

      <p>
        Unlike the Rand family, variation of information is zero exactly when the
        two partitions coincide and increases as the groupings diverge. On the
        space of partitions it satisfies the triangle inequality. When <m>n=0</m>,
        there is no co-placed set on which to compare groupings, so <m>VI</m>
        should be treated as missing.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-five-card-variant">
      <title>A Larger Worked Example</title>

      <p>
        The four-card running question is deliberately too small for stable
        pairwise indices: its largest key group is a pair, and a single moved card
        can swing every index. To make the calculations visible, this subsection
        enlarges the Triangle category by one card. The example is a hypothetical
        snapshot on a variant question, not pilot data, and it does not change the
        four-card running example used elsewhere.
      </p>

      <p>
        Add an isosceles triangle to the shapes question, listed with the other
        triangles. In source order the cards are <m>c_1</m> (equilateral triangle),
        <m>c_2</m> (right triangle), <m>c_3</m> (isosceles triangle), <m>c_4</m>
        (square), and <m>c_5</m> (circle), giving <m>P=5</m>. The zones are
        unchanged: <m>R=3</m>, with Pentagon <m>z_3</m> still a
        <xref ref="gt-decoy-slot" text="custom">decoy slot</xref> and the circle
        still a decoy card. The key sends all three triangles to <m>z_1</m>:
        <md xml:id="an-five-card-key">
          s^\star = (1,1,1,2,0),
          \qquad
          X^\star =
          \begin{bmatrix}
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
            1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Consider the hypothetical checked configuration
        <md>
          s = (1,1,2,2,3).
        </md>
        The student keeps two triangles in Triangle, drops the isosceles triangle
        in with the square, and parks the circle in the empty Pentagon. Three of
        the five cards sit in their home zones, so <m>\sigma(X)=3</m> and
        <m>d^\star(X)=2</m>. The confusion matrix and its slot block submatrix are
        <md xml:id="an-five-card-confusion">
          M(X) =
          \begin{bmatrix}
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
            1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          N(X) =
          \begin{bmatrix}
            2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
        The table shows no omissions and one decoy-card leak, <m>M_{30}=1</m>,
        the circle in the decoy slot. In the slot block, the second row has 
        non-zero entries on two columns, so there is one merge. The first column 
        has non-zero entries on two rows, so there is one split. Thus
        <m>E_{\operatorname{merge}}=E_{\operatorname{split}}=1</m>. Relabeling the
        slots gains nothing: <m>\widetilde{\sigma}(X)=\sigma(X)=3</m>, so the
        labeling gap is zero. This response is wrong in its grouping, not in its
        names.
      </p>

      <p>
        The co-placed cards are <m>\{c_1,c_2,c_3,c_4\}</m>, since the key leaves
        the circle in the tray. Thus <m>n=4</m> and <m>\binom{4}{2}=6</m>. The
        <xref ref="an-pair-together-counts" text="custom">together-counts</xref> are
        <md>
          T_X=\binom{2}{2}+\binom{2}{2}=2,
          \qquad
          T_\star=\binom{3}{2}+\binom{1}{2}=3,
          \qquad
          T_{X\star}=\binom{2}{2}=1.
        </md>
        There is one merge pair, two split pairs, and three agreement pairs. The
        Rand index is therefore
        <md>
          RI(X)=\frac{3}{6}=\frac{1}{2}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Taken alone, <m>RI=\tfrac12</m> sounds like moderate agreement. But with
        these block sizes, the expected together-in-both count is
        <m>T_XT_\star/\binom{n}{2}=2\cdot 3/6=1</m>, exactly what was observed.
        Hence
        <md>
          ARI(X)
          =
          \frac{1-1}{\tfrac{1}{2}(2+3)-1}
          =
          0.
        </md>
        On the entropy scale, the response blocks have sizes <m>(2,2)</m> and the
        key blocks have sizes <m>(3,1)</m>. In bits,
        <md>
          H(\Pi)=1,
          \qquad
          H(\Pi^\star)\approx 0.811,
          \qquad
          I(\Pi;\Pi^\star)\approx 0.311,
        </md>
        so
        <md>
          VI(\Pi,\Pi^\star)
          \approx
          1.189
          \text{ bits}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        This example shows why the advanced diagnostics are not redundant with the
        baseline score. The response has a per-card score of <m>3/5</m>, but its
        adjusted grouping agreement is at the chance baseline. The swapped-slots
        response of <xref ref="subsec-label-free-example" text="title"/> fails in
        the opposite direction: a poor labeled score hides a perfect grouping. The
        two diagnostics reveal different structure.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-pairwise-metric-choice">
      <title>Choosing and Trusting a Pairwise Metric</title>

      <p>
        Among the three indices, the Rand index is easiest to explain but can be
        inflated by separated pairs. The adjusted Rand index is the default choice
        for comparing items or cohorts with different group sizes. Variation of
        information is preferable when a true distance between groupings is needed,
        for instance in clustering response patterns.
      </p>

      <p>
        All three are unstable on a single small answer. The worked example above
        rests on four co-placed cards and six pairs; one moved card changes every
        index. These metrics are most useful at cohort scale, pooled or compared
        across many students and items. They must be computed from per-student
        configurations, not from the placement heatmap
        (<xref ref="subsec-linear-summaries-from-heatmaps" text="title"/>). As with
        label-free agreement, they should be reported only when grouping structure
        is part of the intended analysis; they do not replace labeled accuracy when
        the category names are essential.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-weighted-and-semantic-distances">
    <title>Weighted and Semantic Distances</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The features so far relaxed which comparisons count as agreement. The final
        family keeps the labeled comparison but changes how much each error costs.
        The baseline distance treats every misplaced card as one unit wrong.
        Weighted distances let some wrong placements cost more than others.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-zone-weighted-distance">
      <title>Zone-Weighted Distance</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>C=(C_{ih})_{0\leq i,h\leq R}</m> be a nonnegative cost matrix on
        zones. The entry <m>C_{ih}</m> is the cost of a card sitting in zone
        <m>z_i</m> when its home zone is <m>z_h</m>. Correct placements have zero
        cost:
        <md>
          C_{hh}=0
          \qquad
          \text{for every } h\in\mathcal{Z}.
        </md>
        Because <m>M_{ih}(X)</m> counts exactly how many cards are in that
        current-home relation, the <term>zone-weighted distance</term> is
        <md xml:id="an-zone-weighted-distance">
          L_C(X,X^\star)
          =
          \sum_{i=0}^{R}\sum_{h=0}^{R} C_{ih}M_{ih}(X).
        </md>
        Taking <m>C_{ih}=1</m> for <m>i\neq h</m> and <m>C_{hh}=0</m> recovers
        the ordinary key distance <m>d^\star(X)</m>. Thus the baseline distance is
        the unit-cost special case.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-card-weighted-distance">
      <title>Card-Weighted Distance</title>

      <p>
        Sometimes the cost of an error depends on the card, not only on the zone.
        Let <m>A=(A_{ij})</m> be a nonnegative card-zone cost matrix, where
        <m>A_{ij}</m> is the cost of placing card <m>j</m> in zone <m>z_i</m>. The
        home placement of each card has zero cost:
        <md>
          A_{s^\star(j),j}=0
          \qquad
          \text{for every } j\in\mathcal{P}.
        </md>
        The <term>card-weighted distance</term> is
        <md xml:id="an-card-weighted-distance">
          L_A^{\operatorname{card}}(X,X^\star)
          =
          \sum_{j=1}^{P}\sum_{i=0}^{R} A_{ij}X_{ij}.
        </md>
        This allows the same wrong zone to carry different penalties for different
        cards.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-semantic-distance">
      <title>Semantic Distance</title>

      <p>
        A weighted distance becomes a <term>semantic distance</term> when the costs
        encode content relationships among categories or cards. A rubric might
        charge less for confusing two closely related mathematical categories than
        for confusing unrelated ones. In the shapes example, a triangle placed in
        Quadrilateral might receive a smaller cost than a circle placed in an
        authored polygon slot, if the analysis treats polygon confusions as closer
        than non-polygon confusions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Semantic costs may come from expert judgment, a rubric, a concept map,
        prior item analysis, or a stated research hypothesis. They are modeling
        choices, not direct measurements. They should not be treated as objective
        facts unless justified separately.
      </p>

      <p>
        A useful normalized version anchors the scale at the
        <xref ref="gt-initial-state" text="custom">initial state</xref>:
        <md xml:id="an-normalized-weighted-score">
          S_C(X)
          =
          1-\frac{L_C(X,X^\star)}{L_C(X_0,X^\star)},
          \qquad
          L_C(X_0,X^\star)\neq 0.
        </md>
        It puts the initial state at <m>0</m> and the key at <m>1</m>, but the
        meaning of intermediate values is determined entirely by the chosen costs.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-weight-choice-warning">
      <title>Interpreting Weights</title>

      <p>
        Weighted distances are powerful because they encode domain knowledge, and
        risky for the same reason. Different cost matrices can rank the same pair
        of student responses in opposite orders. The cost matrix is part of the
        result and must be reported with it.
      </p>

      <p>
        In a methods study, the unweighted distance should remain the baseline.
        Weighted and semantic distances are extensions whose interpretation stands
        or falls with the stated weighting scheme.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-advanced-feature-summary">
    <title>Summary</title>

    <p>
      The features of this chapter extend the baseline comparison in three
      directions. Label-free agreement preserves the tray boundary but ignores slot
      names. Partition views, merge and split counts, and pairwise indices compare
      grouping structure. Weighted and semantic distances keep the labeled
      comparison but let errors carry unequal costs.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-advanced-feature-summary">
      <title>Summary of advanced comparison features.</title>
      <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
        <col width="28%" halign="left"/>
        <col width="34%" halign="left"/>
        <col width="38%" halign="left"/>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Feature family</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Question answered</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Use when</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>label-free agreement <m>\widetilde{\sigma}</m>, gap <m>\widetilde{\sigma}-\sigma</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Would renaming the slots make the response correct?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The analysis needs to separate naming errors from grouping failures.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>partition views, slot block <m>N(X)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Which cards are grouped together?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The item or research question concerns grouping structure.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>merge and split counts</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Were key groups combined or divided, and where?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The analysis needs named structural grouping errors.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>pairwise indices <m>RI</m>, <m>ARI</m>, <m>VI</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>How similar are two groupings as a single number?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Grouping agreement is being summarized at cohort scale.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>weighted distances <m>L_C</m>, <m>L_A^{\operatorname{card}}</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>How costly is the response under a chosen rubric?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Errors are deliberately weighted unequally.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      All of these features are optional additions to the framework. The labeled
      baseline features remain the default description of correctness because they
      match what the grader evaluated and what the student saw. The features here
      should be introduced only when the <xref ref="gt-item-design" text="custom">item design</xref>
      or research question calls for a more flexible comparison, and each use
      should state which comparison was made.
    </p>

    <p>
      With the feature vocabulary now complete, the framework turns from
      description to inference. The next chapter, <xref ref="ch-statistical-modeling"/>,
      treats these features as covariates and outcomes in a statistical modeling
      layer and asks whether the patterns they describe are stable across students,
      items, and semesters.
    </p>
  </section>
</chapter>
